doc.add_paragraph('Model Dependent Sensitivity to Semantic Feature Group Outages in Network Intrusion Detection','Title')
p('Mazin Mohammed Hamid\nNorthern Technical University, Iraq\nCorresponding author: mazin.mohammed@ntu.edu.iq')
h('Abstract')
p('Deleting a related family of network measurements need not have the same effect as deleting equally many random columns. We compare four semantic feature-family outages with size-matched random, information-matched and information-plus-correlation-matched controls on RT-IoT2022 and a separate-day corrected LYCOS capture. Twelve grouped RT partitions evaluate twelve fixed configurations; expanded controls use five representative configurations. Under interarrival loss, the semantic-minus-uniform Macro-F1 contrast is −0.1006 for median-imputed random forests but +0.0970 for a clean multilayer perceptron. Opposite model directions persist against matched controls and in AUROC and AP. On Thursday, attack-subtype shift and MLP collapse to all-benign predictions in eleven of twelve interarrival-loss samples require a threshold-dependent interpretation; no SQL-injection inference is supported. Ranking outcomes help distinguish operating-point failure from ranking damage, although average precision also declines under semantic loss. Analyses beyond the first 48 contrasts are exploratory. Expanded Macro-F1 comparisons use pooled Holm adjustment; intervals are corrected pointwise, and secondary ranking contrasts are descriptive. The evidence supports model-dependent and comparator-dependent sensitivity, not a universal tendency for random outages to underestimate damage. Laboratory data, imperfect matching and approximate dependence correction limit generalization.')
p('Keywords: network intrusion detection; missing features; semantic outages; calibration; temporal evaluation; reproducibility.')
h('1 Introduction')
p('An intrusion detector depends on measurements supplied by the network monitoring pipeline. Early intrusion detection models established the role of observable behavior '+cite('denning')+', while subsequent work emphasized the difficulty of translating benchmark performance into operational detection '+cite('sommer')+'. A model can classify complete feature vectors accurately and still fail when a related set of measurements becomes unavailable. The identity of the lost information therefore deserves explicit evaluation.')
p('Surveys of machine learning intrusion detection and public datasets document substantial variation in data generation and evaluation practice '+cite('buczak','ring')+'. Corruption benchmarks and predictive uncertainty studies provide a broader motivation for separating clean performance from behavior under distribution shift '+cite('hendrycks','ovadia')+'. For network tables, however, independently deleting arbitrary columns may not represent the loss of a calculation family such as packet interarrival statistics. Equal numbers of deleted columns need not remove equal amounts or kinds of information.')
p('The contribution is a controlled empirical comparison of model-dependent sensitivity to four feature families: interarrival statistics, payload statistics, activity and idle statistics, and TCP flag counters. Equal outage cardinality alone does not equalize information loss or redundancy. Accordingly, the expanded analysis also compares training-only information-matched and information-plus-correlation-matched column groups. The feature families simulate related information losses; they are not verified independently failing modules in a deployed extractor. Even the added controls do not identify a causal effect of semantic membership.')
p('The primary question is whether semantic loss changes Macro-F1 relative to size-matched random loss within the same data partition and model. Secondary questions examine whether native NaN support, imputation, or augmentation changes this sensitivity; whether apparent noise robustness survives clipping and rounding controls; and whether clean-data probability calibration transfers to damaged inputs. A temporal case tests the same outage definitions on a corrected CICIDS2017 extraction. The study does not claim a new classifier, a universal augmentation benefit, or a direct reproduction of unavailable methods.')
h('2 Related Work')
h('2.1 Dataset quality and evaluation boundaries',2)
p('NSL-KDD and UNSW-NB15 are established network detection benchmarks '+cite('nsl','unsw')+', while CICIDS2017 supplies recorded attack schedules and flow features '+cite('cic2017')+'. Dataset audits identified extraction and labeling problems in the CICIDS2017 family '+cite('engelen','liu')+'. Rosay et al. regenerated the capture as LYCOS-IDS2017 '+cite('lycos')+'. This study uses that author-hosted corrected extraction and its labeling predicates; it does not substitute the distinct Engelen or Liu releases. CICIoT2023 provides a newer IoT benchmark '+cite('ciciot')+', but it is not evaluated here. The temporal extension therefore addresses the corrected-dataset option, not a claim of evaluation on contemporary traffic.')
p('RT-IoT2022 is linked to work on a resource-constrained quantized autoencoder '+cite('rtiotpaper')+'. The dataset is acquired directly from the institutional UCI source '+cite('uci')+'. Numerical representation changes and deduplication are documented because leakage and inappropriate evaluation can invalidate security claims '+cite('leakage','arp')+'. Grouping exact numerical vectors addresses one identifiable overlap mechanism; it does not establish independent hosts, sessions, or sites.')
h('2.2 Missing data and corruption training',2)
p('Rubin distinguishes assumptions concerning missingness mechanisms '+cite('rubin')+'. Chained-equation, nearest-neighbor, random-forest and generative imputation offer different ways to estimate absent values '+cite('mice','knnimpute','missforest','gain')+'. Missing-value handling has also been studied directly in intrusion detection '+cite('missingids')+'. The present outages are deliberately imposed stress conditions, not estimates of a natural missingness process. Median, a budget-limited kNN imputer, and iterative imputation are compared without claiming that they reconstruct the original lost measurements.')
p('MaskAug is the closest identified corruption-aware intrusion detection method '+cite('maskaug')+'. Its bibliographic metadata and public abstract were checked. The publisher full text was access restricted, and no open version was identified during preparation. The abstract does not specify enough implementation detail to establish equivalence of masking schedules, noise settings, and training configurations. Consequently, the independent 30% masking configurations in this study are labeled generic baselines, not MaskAug reimplementations. No numerical superiority claim is made against MaskAug, and a full reproduction remains outstanding until the complete specification can be examined.')
p('VIME uses self- and semi-supervised objectives for tabular learning '+cite('vime')+', and TabNet provides an attentive tabular architecture '+cite('tabnet')+'. Broader comparisons caution against assuming that deep learning dominates tabular data '+cite('shwartz','gorishniy','grinsztajn')+'. An MLP supplies a neural comparator here, while random forests and gradient boosting provide the executed tree ensemble families '+cite('rf','gb')+'. The fixed model settings permit direct evaluation of outage patterns but do not establish a tuned architecture ranking.')
h('2.3 Probability quality and statistical comparisons',2)
p('ROC and precision-recall analysis measure complementary aspects of ranking '+cite('fawcett','davis','saito')+'. Probability calibration is a separate property from ranking or threshold accuracy '+cite('niculescu','guo')+'. Accordingly, this study reports AUROC and average precision, raw and calibrated Brier scores, and expected calibration error, together with fixed and calibration-selected decision thresholds. Average precision is the non-interpolated scikit-learn statistic, not a trapezoidal precision-recall area.')
p('Repeated evaluation requires attention to dependence. Demšar discusses comparisons across datasets '+cite('demsar')+', but repeated partitions of one dataset do not become independent datasets. Corrected resampled inference '+cite('nadeau')+' is used as an approximate adjustment, and its limitations are acknowledged in light of variance-estimation difficulties '+cite('grandvalet')+'. Holm correction controls the 48-comparison primary family '+cite('holm')+'. These procedures do not create independent network environments from repeated partitions.')
h('3 Materials and Methods')
h('3.1 Source data and leakage controls',2)
p('RT-IoT2022 was downloaded from the official UCI CSV endpoint. The source contains 123,117 rows. Normal labels are Thing_Speak, MQTT_Publish, and Wipro_bulb; all other observed labels map to attack. The representation retains 79 numerical predictors after excluding identifiers and source/destination ports; protocol, service, and the target are excluded. Exact duplicates of the pair consisting of the numerical vector and binary target are removed, leaving 18,270 rows and 18,262 unique input vectors. Contradictory target assignments are retained: eight numerical-vector groups have both targets. The 2,010 original rows belonging to conflicting groups are not discarded wholesale.')
p('Exact contiguous float64 row bytes define numerical-vector groups. Twelve label-blind random group partitions use seeds 1001–1012 and proportions 60/20/20 for training, calibration, and testing. Rows are then sampled within each partition, stratified by binary target, to caps of 6,000/1,500/3,000. Identical numerical vectors cannot cross the three partitions. Sampling uses labels retrospectively and changes benchmark composition; it is not a prospective traffic sampling process. All numerical preprocessing is fitted on training rows only. The repeated partitions overlap with each other, and seed changes jointly affect partitions, sampling, estimator randomness and corruption, rather than isolating these variance components.')
p('The Tuesday temporal source is the complete Tuesday-WorkingHours.pcap_lycos.zip archive from the LYCOS authors’ university repository. Its 42,118,313 bytes pass ZIP CRC verification; SHA-256 is f86c47defd159772dc717ce5b717369c04d0ad460d4e76fd6056ec38fbaa7405. All 319,637 rows pass finite-feature and nonnegative-duration screening; 6,962 are attacks after applying the original Tuesday address-and-time predicates. Labels are reconstructed from those predicates, not from row order. The archive, labeling-code URL and digests are recorded in the release. IPs, ports, flow identifiers, protocol, timestamp and label are excluded, leaving 75 numerical predictors.')
caption('Table 1  Experimental populations and evaluation scope')
table(['Setting','Features','Partition structure','Fitted classifiers'],[['RT-IoT2022','79','12 group splits; 6000 / 1500 / 3000 rows','144'],['LYCOS Tuesday','75','3 temporal windows × 12 seeds; same caps','144'],['RT control refits','79','12 original partitions; 5 configurations','60'],['LYCOS Thursday','75','1 temporal construction × 12 seeds; 5 configurations','60']],[1.3,.65,3.9,.95])
p('For the temporal case, training ends at 12:30, 12:40 or 12:50 UTC on 4 July 2017. The next ten minutes form calibration and the following fifteen minutes form testing. Flow end is timestamp plus flow_duration, both in microseconds. Training flow ends precede the cutoff; calibration flows start after it and end before the calibration boundary; test flows start after that boundary and end before the test boundary. Thus no selected flow crosses an adjacent partition boundary. Twelve stratified samples per window use the same size caps. The windows overlap, cover one Tuesday capture and benign/FTP brute-force behavior, and do not establish host independence or coverage of all attack classes.')
h('3.2 Semantic and matched random outages',2)
p('Let X be a test feature matrix and G a fixed feature family. The semantic operator replaces every entry in columns G with NaN. Its comparator draws a uniform subset R of the retained columns with |R| = |G|, and likewise removes those columns for every test row. Three independent random subsets per family are evaluated in each primary split; two are evaluated in each temporal window/seed. The matched-random score is averaged before paired differences are computed. Draws are without replacement inside a subset; chance overlap with the semantic family is allowed. Corrupted matrices are shared across methods within each split.')
caption('Table 2  Semantic families and exact outage cardinalities')
tg=json.load(open(R/'temporal_results/groups.json'))
table(['Family','RT columns','LYCOS columns','Feature-name rule'],[[g,len(v),len(tg[g]),{'interarrival':'_iat. or iat_','payload':'_pkts_payload. or pkt_len_','activity':'active. / idle. or active_ / idle_','flags':'flag_count or flag_'}[g]] for g,v in manifest['semantic_groups'].items()],[1.1,.8,1.0,3.9])
p('These groups represent related numerical calculations, not verified modules with separate failure domains. Other related predictors remain available, so outages can preserve redundant information. The precise column lists in the manifest, rather than descriptive names alone, define the experiment. Missingness is imposed after splitting and does not change test labels.')
h('3.3 Classifiers and preprocessing',2)
p('The experiments use scikit-learn '+cite('sklearn')+' with fixed settings and no test-informed hyperparameter tuning. Random forests have 60 trees and maximum depth 18. Histogram boosting uses 80 iterations, 15 maximum leaves and disabled early stopping. The MLP has hidden widths 64 and 32, batch size 256, at most 100 iterations and internal early stopping. Median imputation and standardization are estimated from unaugmented training rows; neural inputs are standardized. Native histogram configurations receive NaN directly without imputation. Software versions and fit warnings are retained in the package.')
caption('Table 3  Primary configurations')
table(['Configuration','Missing-value treatment','Training modification'],[['RF_median','Training medians','None'],['RF_repeat','Training medians','Two identical clean copies'],['RF_mask','Training medians','Clean plus 30% masked copy'],['RF_noise','Training medians','Clean plus Gaussian noisy copy'],['Hist_median','Training medians','None'],['Hist_native','Native NaN branches','None'],['Hist_native_mask','Native NaN branches','Clean plus 30% masked copy'],['RF_knn','5 neighbors; 256 reference rows','None'],['RF_iterative','3 rounds; 5 nearest predictors','None'],['MLP_clean','Training medians and scaling','None'],['MLP_mask','Training medians and scaling','Clean plus 30% masked copy'],['MLP_noise','Training medians and scaling','Clean plus Gaussian noisy copy']],[1.45,2.5,2.85])
p('The kNN imputer operates on standardized predictors and uses the first 256 selected training rows as its reference set, with five neighbors. It is a reference-capped computational comparator, not full-training kNN imputation. Iterative imputation also uses standardized predictors, three rounds, five nearest predictors and the default Bayesian-ridge estimator, with skip_complete disabled. The short iteration budget may restrict convergence. This comparison does not establish that iterative imputation is generally inferior; warnings are disclosed rather than suppressed.')
p('Masking adds one independently masked training copy, with cell probability 0.30. Noise training adds one copy with independent zero-mean Gaussian perturbation of standard deviation 0.1 times each training column’s standard deviation. Training noise is unbounded. RF_repeat controls for duplicated row count and tree count; it does not equalize elapsed time. MLP augmentation changes the number of optimization updates and is not update-budget matched. Histogram native NaN handling is evaluated both without and with masked training exposure, since native support alone need not learn a useful response to unseen missing patterns.')
h('3.4 Noise controls and probability evaluation',2)
p('The primary noise ablation uses multipliers 0.1 and 0.3 and five operators: noise only; clipping only; noise followed by clipping; clipping followed by integer rounding; and noise followed by clipping and integer rounding. Bounds are training minima and maxima. Integer-valued columns are detected using training values only; zero-variance columns receive zero random perturbation. Both noise multipliers share the same standard-normal test matrix within a split. Clipping-only and projection-only evaluations are repeated across multiplier labels and are not independent observations. Per-column bounds and rounding do not enforce joint packet or flow constraints.')
p('Every primary fit is evaluated on 27 conditions: clean data, four semantic losses, twelve matched random losses and ten noise/control cases. Every temporal fit uses thirteen conditions: clean, four semantic losses and eight matched random losses. Recorded metrics include Macro-F1, attack recall, false-positive rate, AUROC, average precision, Brier score and ten equal-width-bin binary expected calibration error. Primary records additionally contain balanced accuracy. Means across semantic families weight families equally and are descriptive summaries, not a population outage risk.')
p('Each fitted classifier produces raw attack probabilities. A logistic Platt model with C = 10⁶ is fitted on clipped probability log-odds from the clean held-out calibration set; clipping uses [10⁻⁶, 1−10⁻⁶]. The decision threshold maximizes Youden J on calibrated probabilities from that same calibration set, with deterministic first-maximum selection. The selected threshold is then frozen for all test corruptions. Raw classification uses 0.5. Neither calibration nor threshold uses test labels, but selecting the threshold on the calibration sample adds finite-sample uncertainty. Calibration is fitted only to clean data and is not adapted after observing damage.')
h('3.5 Statistical analysis',2)
p('For each method and family, the primary paired difference is semantic Macro-F1 minus the mean of three same-size random-outage Macro-F1 values in the same split, all at the raw 0.5 threshold. Twelve split differences define the estimate. Because resampled train/test sets overlap, the corrected resampled t approximation uses SE = sqrt[(1/12 + n_test/n_train) s_d²], n_test/n_train = 0.5, and 11 degrees of freedom. Two-sided p-values are adjusted jointly across all 12 × 4 = 48 comparisons using Holm. Corrected pointwise 95% intervals use the same SE and are not simultaneous confidence intervals.')
p('This correction is a heuristic for dependence induced by resampling. It does not model all dependence caused by the grouped partition construction, subsampling or repeated corruption, nor does it support inference across independent deployments. Its intervals are much wider than naive standard errors over individual rows. Secondary augmentation, noise, calibration and temporal comparisons are descriptive and are not presented as additional confirmatory significance tests. The temporal windows overlap and share a capture day; their seed standard deviations must not be interpreted as independent temporal uncertainty.')
h('3.6 Expanded controls and separate day replication',2)
p('The expanded control analysis evaluates information content and redundancy as alternative explanations and is treated as exploratory. It evaluates RF_median, RF_mask, Hist_median, Hist_native and MLP_clean on the original twelve primary partitions. Models are refitted with their original seeds and preprocessing; semantic-outage scores are verified against the original records. Each family now has twenty uniformly sampled outage subsets, twenty subsets approximately matched in marginal label information, and twenty subsets matched jointly in information and within-group correlation. These are additional tests of sensitivity, not a relabeling of the original 48-comparison analysis.')
p('For each training partition, every numerical predictor is discretized using unique decile boundaries and its mutual information with the binary training target is calculated. A group descriptor comprises the sum of these marginal mutual informations, its mean absolute pairwise Spearman correlation, and the fraction of exactly constant training columns. A pool of 20,000 random subsets of the required cardinality is generated and deduplicated; the exact semantic set is excluded. Information matching selects the twenty candidates with the smallest sum of squared standardized information and constant-fraction discrepancies. Joint matching adds the standardized correlation discrepancy. Standardization uses the candidate-pool standard deviation with a numerical floor. Selection never uses calibration or test labels.')
p('The twenty selected sets are approximate nearest matches, not independent semantic interventions. They may overlap each other and the semantic family. Matching marginal information does not control conditional importance or higher-order interactions; matching one correlation summary does not equalize the dependence structure. Supplementary Table S3 reports residual discrepancies. Candidate selection and additional control definitions were chosen after examining the original experiment, so this analysis is exploratory.')
p('A separate replication uses the complete official LYCOS Thursday archive and the original Thursday labeling predicates. The authors exclude afternoon traffic from this corrected release. Training uses flows ending before 12:45 UTC on 6 July 2017; calibration uses flows starting at or after 12:45 and ending before 13:20; testing uses flows starting at or after 13:20 and ending before 14:00. The same 6000/1500/3000 caps, twelve seeds, five configurations and twenty subsets per comparator are applied. The test covers XSS and SQL-injection periods after training on the earlier web brute-force period. This extends attack and capture-day coverage within CICIDS2017, without becoming an independent network or contemporary collection.')
p('Each dataset contributes sixty exploratory contrasts: five methods by four families by three comparators. The same corrected resampled t approximation is applied to split-level semantic-minus-comparator differences. Holm correction is applied jointly across all 120 expanded contrasts from both datasets. Corrected intervals remain pointwise. Candidate draws are averaged within each split, never treated as independent training runs. Probability metrics are recorded for the new controls, while their contrast tests use the raw 0.5 threshold. The temporal replication uses one chronological construction resampled twelve times; its uncertainty is conditional on that capture and sampling design.')
h('3.7 Predictor variability and replacement diagnostics',2)
p('A feature audit reports exact-constant columns and near-constant columns, defined by at least 99% modal frequency, separately within every selected training and test partition. This avoids inferring degeneracy from unchanged Macro-F1. A further descriptive analysis refits RF_median and MLP_clean on both datasets with the original seeds. Every predictor is replaced individually by its training median. Family-level semantic and twenty-draw uniform outages are also evaluated with training-median versus training-mean replacement while leaving each fitted classifier and preprocessing transform fixed. For the standardized MLP, the latter is exactly zero in standardized coordinates. The comparison diagnoses sensitivity to the replacement point, not a new training method or a causal field failure.')
p('The diagnostic stage makes 84 fits/refits: sixty RT clean rechecks across all five representative configurations, and 24 Thursday refits of RF_median and MLP_clean. The forty-eight RF/MLP diagnostic fits produce 8064 family/control replacement evaluations and 3696 single-column evaluations. The 84 clean rechecks are recorded separately. A subsequent operating-point audit makes twelve additional Thursday MLP refits and sixty clean/semantic evaluations, reproducing the existing benchmark scores; these are diagnostic checks, not new independent environments. The payload-membership stratification reuses existing scores and requires no additional fits. Clean and median-family scores are checked against the corresponding benchmark records. These post hoc diagnostics are descriptive; no prospective registration or new confirmatory p-value family is claimed. Full results appear in Supplementary Tables S6–S12.')
h('4 Results')
h('4.1 Semantic information loss',2)
p('The original experiment completed 144 fits and 3,888 evaluations. Four of 48 contrasts have Holm-adjusted p-values below 0.05 (Table 4); the RF_median interarrival result is borderline at 0.049684 and is not treated as a robust universal finding. Direction matters: clean MLP has a positive interarrival contrast, whereas the corresponding tree contrasts are negative. The results therefore support model-dependent sensitivity with limited adjusted evidence, rather than a general claim that random outages underestimate damage. Nonsignificance does not establish equivalence.')
fig('semantic_gap.png','Figure 1. Semantic minus size-matched random Macro-F1 for eight selected configurations. Bars are corrected pointwise 95% intervals over twelve splits; they are not simultaneous intervals. All 48 tests are reported in Supplementary Table S1.')
caption('Table 4  Primary comparisons with Holm adjusted p below 0.05')
sig=stats[stats.p_holm<.05]
table(['Method and family','Difference','Corrected 95% interval','Holm p'],[[r.method+' / '+r.family,fmt(r.difference_semantic_minus_random),'['+fmt(r.ci95_lower)+', '+fmt(r.ci95_upper)+']',f'{r.p_holm:.6f}'] for r in sig.itertuples()],[2.35,.8,2.7,.95])
p('For RF_median, interarrival loss yields mean Macro-F1 0.8760 and a semantic-minus-random contrast of −0.1012, with corrected pointwise interval [−0.1521, −0.0503]. This example is one of a heterogeneous set. RF_repeat also has a negative interarrival contrast; noise-trained RF and masked MLP have negative payload contrasts. By contrast, other configurations show positive effects for some families. These patterns cannot be attributed to semantic identity alone because the comparator groups differ in label information, redundancy and constant-column content.')
p('Table 5 separates clean classification from averages over the four semantic outages. Averaging is useful for compact comparison but can conceal the severe family-specific losses shown in Figure 2. Native histogram boosting achieves an outage mean of '+fmt(m[(m.method=='Hist_native')&(m.scenario=='semantic')].fixed_f1.mean())+', whereas adding independent masked training yields '+fmt(m[(m.method=='Hist_native_mask')&(m.scenario=='semantic')].fixed_f1.mean())+'. This is a comparison of these fixed configurations, not a tuned architecture ranking.')
caption('Table 5  RT IoT performance averaged over four semantic outages')
sem=m[m.scenario=='semantic'].groupby('method').mean(numeric_only=True)
table(['Method','Clean F1','Outage F1','AUROC','AP','Brier raw','Brier cal'],[[method,fmt(val(method)),fmt(sem.loc[method,'fixed_f1']),fmt(sem.loc[method,'auroc']),fmt(sem.loc[method,'ap']),fmt(sem.loc[method,'brier_raw']),fmt(sem.loc[method,'brier_calibrated'])] for method in manifest['methods']],[1.55,.85,.9,.8,.8,.95,.95])
fig('method_comparison.png','Figure 2. Mean fixed-threshold Macro-F1 on clean RT-IoT2022 data and each semantic outage. Panels group related fixed configurations; they do not imply equal tuning or neural update budgets.')
h('4.2 Noise and projection ablations',2)
p('Noise tolerance depends on training and the test operator. At multiplier 0.3, RF_repeat reaches Macro-F1 '+fmt(val('RF_repeat','noise_only','0.3'))+' under unbounded noise, compared with '+fmt(val('RF_noise','noise_only','0.3'))+' for RF_noise. MLP_clean and MLP_noise reach '+fmt(val('MLP_clean','noise_only','0.3'))+' and '+fmt(val('MLP_noise','noise_only','0.3'))+', respectively. These descriptive differences support including a noise-trained competitor before attributing robustness to another augmentation technique.')
p('Clipping is not a neutral implementation detail. For MLP_clean at multiplier 0.3, unbounded noise gives '+fmt(val('MLP_clean','noise_only','0.3'))+', while noise followed by clipping gives '+fmt(val('MLP_clean','clip_noise','0.3'))+'. The clipping-only control gives '+fmt(val('MLP_clean','clip_only','0.3'))+'. Comparing all three prevents a gain or loss caused by projection from being assigned entirely to random noise. Rounding adds another intervention on integer-valued predictors. No operator is asserted to reproduce a physical network fault.')
fig('noise_ablation.png','Figure 3. Grouped-bar comparison of unordered noise and projection interventions on RT-IoT2022. The same standard-normal perturbation is scaled within a split. Clipping-only and projection-only controls repeated across panels are the same condition, not additional independent observations.')
h('4.3 Probabilities and frozen thresholds',2)
p('All configurations have AUROC and average precision recorded under every tested corruption. High ranking performance does not ensure stable classification at 0.5: RF_noise averages AUROC '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_noise','auroc'])+' over semantic outages but Macro-F1 '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_noise','fixed_f1'])+'. The release reports recall and false-positive rate as well as ranking statistics so that operating behavior can be assessed.')
p('Platt scaling fitted to clean calibration data is not uniformly beneficial under outage. For RF_median, average semantic Brier changes from '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_median','brier_raw'])+' to '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_median','brier_calibrated'])+'; for RF_iterative it changes from '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_iterative','brier_raw'])+' to '+fmt(sem.loc['RF_iterative','brier_calibrated'])+'. The frozen calibration-selected threshold can also improve or worsen Macro-F1. These results argue for measuring probability quality under the actual degradation instead of assuming that clean-data calibration transfers unchanged.')
fig('probability_thresholds.png','Figure 4. Raw and calibrated probability quality and threshold behavior averaged equally across four semantic outages. Lower Brier is better; higher Macro-F1 is better. Thresholds and Platt models use only clean held-out calibration records.')
h('4.4 Temporal corrected capture case',2)
p('The complete official Tuesday archive produces 144 temporal fits and 1,872 evaluation records. All 36 window/seed partitions satisfy maximum training flow end < minimum calibration flow start and maximum calibration flow end < minimum test flow start. Table 6 reports four baseline methods in each window. This extension is intentionally narrower than the primary twelve-configuration comparison: it tests the outage contrast across historical time boundaries and does not validate augmentation transfer on every attack class.')
caption('Table 6  Temporal case means over twelve seeds and four semantic outages')
rows=[]
for w in range(3):
 for method in ['RF_median','Hist_median','Hist_native','MLP_clean']:
  q=tm[(tm.window==w)&(tm.method==method)];v=q[q.scenario=='semantic'].mean(numeric_only=True);clean=q[q.scenario=='clean'].macro_f1.iloc[0]
  rows.append([['12:40–12:55','12:50–13:05','13:00–13:15'][w],method,fmt(clean),fmt(v.macro_f1),fmt(v.auroc),fmt(v.ap)])
table(['Test UTC','Method','Clean F1','Outage F1','AUROC','AP'],rows,[1.15,1.5,1.05,1.1,1.0,1.0])
p('Near-perfect AUROC in several temporal conditions coexists with lower fixed-threshold Macro-F1. This indicates that ranking and operating thresholds can respond differently even within a restricted temporal test. The case is relatively easy on clean FTP/benign traffic and cannot be interpreted as evidence of general temporal deployment robustness. Full per-family probabilities, seed standard deviations and matched-random gaps are reported in Supplementary Table S5 rather than pooled into an independent capture-level significance test.')
fig('temporal.png','Figure 5. Fixed-threshold Macro-F1 by outage family in three strictly separated training/calibration/test temporal constructions. The three test windows overlap and belong to one capture day; lines show seed means, not independent temporal replications.')
h('4.5 Information and redundancy controls',2)
p('Both expanded datasets contain 245 conditions per fitted model: one clean condition, four semantic outages, and 240 comparator outages from four families, three comparator types and twenty draws. Each expanded table contains 14,700 records. Clean scores were checked against the corresponding original evaluations. Outage contrasts use only semantic and comparator conditions; clean rows are reported separately for reference. Supplementary Tables S1–S12 accompany this manuscript as a separate document, with full electronic metric tables supplied in the research package.')
expanded=pd.read_csv(R/'results/expanded_statistics_120.csv');crt=expanded[expanded.dataset=='control_results'];cth=expanded[expanded.dataset=='thursday_controls']
p('The expanded RT-IoT2022 experiment completes sixty refits and 14,700 evaluations. Across the 120 expanded exploratory contrasts, sixteen have pooled Holm-adjusted p-values below 0.05: ten on RT-IoT2022 and six on Thursday. These findings include both positive and negative effects. Some Thursday findings involve comparisons affected by the documented MLP threshold collapse. For example, the activity-versus-uniform Macro-F1 advantage arises because semantic outages leave decisions intact while comparator outages degrade the operating point. They cannot all be interpreted as preserved ranking or stable outage robustness (Sections 4.6 and 4.8). Semantic scores reproduce the corresponding original values exactly. Increasing uniform draws from three to twenty changes the precision and point estimates of the random comparator, so the new results are reported as an exploratory extension rather than replacing the original p-values. Table 7 shows the interarrival contrasts across all five representative configurations; Supplementary Table S2 contains all 120 expanded comparisons.')
caption('Table 7  RT IoT interarrival contrasts against expanded controls')
rows=[]
for method in ['RF_median','RF_mask','Hist_median','Hist_native','MLP_clean']:
 for control,label in [('uniform','Uniform'),('importance','Information'),('importance_correlation','Information and correlation')]:
  q=crt[(crt.method==method)&(crt.family=='interarrival')&(crt.control==control)].iloc[0]
  rows.append([method,label,fmt(q.difference),'['+fmt(q.ci95_lower)+', '+fmt(q.ci95_upper)+']',f'{q.p_holm_global_120:.4f}'])
table(['Method','Comparator','Difference','Pointwise 95% interval','Holm p (n=120)'],rows,[1.25,1.7,.8,2.15,.9])
p('With twenty uniform draws, clean MLP has an interarrival contrast of +0.0970, while median-imputed RF has −0.1006. Against the joint information-and-correlation comparator these become +0.0960 and −0.0899, respectively. Opposite point-estimate directions therefore persist in these examples after additional control. The RF joint-control comparison has pooled adjusted p = 0.0537, whereas the MLP joint-control comparison has p = 0.0151; the two must not be described as equally strong statistical evidence. This is a model-dependent pattern, not evidence that semantic membership has an intrinsic harmful effect. Histogram-boosting payload contrasts can also reverse direction relative to information-matched groups, illustrating the dependence on comparator construction.')
diag=pd.read_csv(R/'control_results/matching_diagnostics.csv');err=diag.groupby('control')[['mi_standardized_error','correlation_standardized_error']].agg(lambda a:np.abs(a).mean())
p('Matching reduces but does not eliminate imbalance. Across RT-IoT2022 families and splits, mean absolute standardized information/correlation discrepancies are '+fmt(err.loc['uniform','mi_standardized_error'])+' / '+fmt(err.loc['uniform','correlation_standardized_error'])+' for uniform subsets, '+fmt(err.loc['importance','mi_standardized_error'])+' / '+fmt(err.loc['importance','correlation_standardized_error'])+' for information matching, and '+fmt(err.loc['importance_correlation','mi_standardized_error'])+' / '+fmt(err.loc['importance_correlation','correlation_standardized_error'])+' for joint matching. The joint control trades some information matching precision for reduced correlation mismatch. Remaining imbalance and unmeasured interactions limit attribution.')
fig('matched_controls.png','Figure 6. RT-IoT2022 semantic-minus-comparator differences using twenty subsets per comparator and five representative configurations. Intervals are corrected pointwise 95% intervals. Significance reporting in Table 7 uses the pooled 120-comparison exploratory Holm family, not the intervals alone.')
h('4.6 Separate Thursday capture replication',2)
th=json.load(open(R/'thursday_results/protocol_manifest.json'));dth=pd.read_csv(R/'thursday_controls/raw.csv');sth=dth[dth.control=='semantic'].groupby(['method','family']).macro_f1.mean();cleanth=dth[dth.control=='clean'].groupby('method').macro_f1.mean()
p('The full Thursday archive contains '+str(th['raw_archive_rows'])+' rows; the corrected morning analysis retains '+str(th['retained_morning_rows'])+'. Source labeling identifies '+str(th['attack_counts'].get('web_bruteforce',0))+' web brute-force, '+str(th['attack_counts'].get('web_xss',0))+' XSS and '+str(th['attack_counts'].get('web_sql',0))+' SQL-injection flows in the retained morning population. Training, calibration and test class counts for every seed are reported in Supplementary Table S4. The chronological replication completes sixty fits and 14,700 evaluations, including clean data and all three control types. It expands the earlier FTP-only case but remains a limited laboratory capture.')
p('The Thursday construction jointly changes capture time, attack subtype and measurement availability. Training contains web brute-force only, whereas testing contains XSS and SQL-injection flows; calibration includes both brute-force and XSS. Consequently, clean-to-damaged comparisons are conditional on a classifier already facing attack-subtype shift and do not isolate temporal drift or unseen-attack generalization. Each stratified training sample contains 142 brute-force flows, but an index-level audit shows that these are different overlapping samples, with 732 distinct attack rows across all twelve seeds. Fixed counts therefore do not imply fixed attack identities. Nevertheless, the seeds condition on one chronological construction and a fixed class allocation, and do not measure uncertainty over independent attack episodes, prevalence, days or sites.')
caption('Table 8  Thursday Macro F1 by outage family')
table(['Method','Clean','IAT','Payload','Activity','Flags'],[[method,fmt(cleanth.loc[method])]+[fmt(sth.loc[(method,g)]) for g in ['interarrival','payload','activity','flags']] for method in ['RF_median','RF_mask','Hist_median','Hist_native','MLP_clean']],[1.6,1.04,1.04,1.04,1.04,1.04])
p('On Thursday, the median-imputed RF interarrival contrast against uniform groups is −0.2542 (pooled adjusted p = 0.00155), whereas the median-imputed histogram payload contrast is +0.1749 (p = 0.01688). The separate-day results and their full contrast distribution are reported in Figure 7 and Supplementary Table S2. They are not pooled with RT-IoT2022 as independent sites. High clean performance, low prevalence of some attacks and only twelve SQL-injection flows in the full retained morning population constrain class-specific inference. Three of the twelve sampled tests contain no SQL-injection flow, so no SQL-specific detection conclusion is drawn. Cross-capture differences are interpreted as scope dependence rather than averaged into a universal effect.')
p('Every selected test contains 66 attacks among 3000 flows (2.2%). Predicting all flows as benign gives Macro-F1 0.4944. A reproducibility audit of twelve MLP refits recovers the reported semantic scores and shows that interarrival loss produces all-benign predictions in eleven seeds; the remaining seed detects only 3 of 66 attacks, with no false positives. Mean attack recall is 0.0038, explaining the mean Macro-F1 of 0.4981. Flag loss gives all-benign predictions in every seed. Thus large contrasts such as the activity-versus-uniform difference of +0.3115 can reflect threshold collapse in the comparator under rare-attack and subtype-shift conditions, rather than a stable general robustness advantage. Full confusion counts and the all-benign reference appear in Supplementary Table S10. No alternate training experiment mixing brute-force and XSS is claimed.')
fig('thursday_controls.png','Figure 7. Expanded outage controls on the strictly separated Thursday temporal test. Bars are corrected pointwise intervals conditional on the twelve samples of one chronological construction. They are not confidence intervals over independent capture days or sites.')
h('4.7 Variability and mechanism diagnostics',2)
p('The Thursday activity family is not degenerate under the stated checks. Across all twelve samples, none of its eight columns is exactly constant or reaches the 99% modal criterion in training or testing. Its mean column modal frequency is 0.8015 in training and 0.8192 in testing. Table 9 reports all families so that an unchanged classification score cannot be confused with a constant input. The flag families do contain constant or near-constant columns, reinforcing the need for the matching control and feature audit.')
caption('Table 9 Mean fraction of constant and near constant columns')
variation=pd.read_csv(R/'mechanism_results/variation_summary.csv');rows=[]
for dataset in ['rt','thursday']:
 for family in ['interarrival','payload','activity','flags']:
  a=variation[(variation.dataset==dataset)&(variation.family==family)].set_index('partition')
  rows.append(['RT' if dataset=='rt' else 'Thursday',family]+[f'{100*a.loc[partition,metric]:.2f}' for metric,partition in [('constant_fraction','train'),('constant_fraction','test'),('near_constant_fraction','train'),('near_constant_fraction','test')]])
table(['Dataset','Family','Train constant %','Test constant %','Train near constant %','Test near constant %'],rows,[.95,1.15,1.15,1.15,1.2,1.2])
p('Under median replacement of Thursday activity columns, RF and MLP change none of their thresholded test predictions across the twelve seeds, but their probabilities do change: mean absolute changes are 0.00576 and 0.00089, respectively. The identical Macro-F1 values therefore reflect threshold-level insensitivity in these configurations, not constant measurements or complete predictive invariance. This low-impact condition is retained for audit and is not treated alone as evidence of fault robustness. Histogram native NaN handling shows a small score change as well; equal family names do not imply equal reliance across models.')
p('Column-level interventions help explain the RT model contrast. For MLP, replacing fwd_pkts_payload.min and flow_pkts_payload.min separately changes mean Macro-F1 by −0.1106 and −0.0965; fwd_header_size_max changes it by −0.0841. At least one of the two payload minima occurs in 89 of 240 interarrival-sized uniform outage subsets. Semantic interarrival loss preserves these predictors, whereas some random subsets remove them. A direct post hoc stratification of these evaluated uniform subsets is reported in Supplementary Table S9. Averaging within membership strata in each seed and then weighting the twelve seeds equally, groups containing either payload minimum (89 draws) have mean Macro-F1 0.8204 and semantic-minus-uniform contrast +0.1396. Groups containing neither (151 draws) have mean Macro-F1 0.8888 and contrast +0.0712. Every seed contains both strata, and the residual contrast remains positive in all twelve seeds (range +0.0349 to +0.1185). Exclusion therefore attenuates but does not eliminate the contrast. The two columns contribute to a model-level explanation but do not fully account for it. These strata differ in other selected columns, are not randomized interventions on membership, and were selected after inspecting single-column effects; they do not establish a causal mechanism or independent confirmatory evidence.')
p('RF behaves differently: its largest mean single-column loss on RT is 0.0076 for flow_iat.min, much smaller than the loss from removing the interarrival family jointly. Thus no single measured column accounts additively for its group sensitivity. The joint removal can eliminate redundant alternatives used by the ensemble; this is a plausible model-level interpretation supported by the joint/single contrast, not proof of a particular extractor fault mechanism.')
caption('Table 10 Replacement point sensitivity of semantic minus uniform contrasts')
rep=pd.read_csv(R/'mechanism_results/replacement_summary.csv');rows=[]
for dataset in ['rt','thursday']:
 for method in ['RF_median','MLP_clean']:
  for family in ['interarrival','payload']:
   q=rep[(rep.dataset==dataset)&(rep.method==method)&(rep.family==family)].set_index('replacement');rows.append(['RT' if dataset=='rt' else 'Thursday',method,family,fmt(q.loc['median','contrast']),fmt(q.loc['mean','contrast'])])
table(['Dataset','Model','Family','Median fill contrast','Mean fill contrast'],rows,[.95,1.4,1.25,1.6,1.6])
p('Changing the replacement point modifies magnitudes and can change directions on Thursday. On RT, the MLP interarrival contrast decreases from +0.0970 to +0.0609 and its payload contrast from −0.1524 to −0.1126, preserving their opposite signs. The replacement point therefore contributes to score sensitivity but is not the sole explanation of the RT direction pattern. StandardScaler was held fixed, so this diagnostic does not isolate the effect of using standardization during training. Raw variance alone is insufficient because StandardScaler normalizes nonconstant training columns; the relevant inserted coordinate is (training median−training mean)/training SD for median filling, versus zero for mean filling. Learned decision geometry, feature content and interactions remain relevant. Supplementary Table S7 reports every family and shows why this explanation is dataset conditional.')
h('4.8 Secondary ranking contrasts',2)
p('AUROC and AP are evaluated on exactly the same held-out flows and the same semantic and comparator outages as Macro-F1. Comparator draws are first averaged within seed, followed by paired semantic-minus-comparator differences across twelve seeds. This secondary descriptive analysis reuses existing predictions and performs no new fitting. Supplementary Table S11 gives all 48 original comparisons and S12 all 120 expanded comparisons, for each of the three outcomes. The reported intervals use the same corrected resampling approximation as the primary analysis and remain pointwise, not multiplicity-adjusted. No secondary p-values, additional confirmatory findings, equivalence tests or cross-site claims are made.')
caption('Table 11  Semantic minus uniform contrasts for threshold and ranking outcomes')
rank=pd.read_csv(R/'results/ranking_contrasts_secondary.csv');sel=rank[(rank.component!='primary_RT')&(rank.control=='uniform')&(rank.method.isin(['RF_median','MLP_clean']))];rr=[]
for dataset in ['expanded_RT','Thursday']:
 for method in ['RF_median','MLP_clean']:
  for family in ['interarrival','payload','activity','flags']:
   z=sel[(sel.component==dataset)&(sel.method==method)&(sel.family==family)].set_index('metric');rr.append(['RT' if dataset=='expanded_RT' else dataset,method,family]+[fmt(z.loc[k,'difference']) for k in ['macro_f1','auroc','ap']])
table(['Dataset','Method','Family','Delta Macro F1','Delta AUROC','Delta AP'],rr,[.7,1.3,1.15,1.05,1.05,1.05])
p('On Thursday, clean MLP AUROC is 0.9833 and the equal-family semantic mean is 0.9840, while AP falls from 0.8202 to 0.7567. The apparent preservation of average AUROC therefore does not establish unchanged rare-attack retrieval, and averaging can hide family-specific behavior. Under interarrival loss, semantic AUROC is 0.9845 versus 0.9743 for uniform loss, and AP is 0.7630 versus 0.6227. Their positive contrasts (+0.0102 and +0.1404) oppose the negative fixed-threshold Macro-F1 contrast (−0.0407). Nevertheless, corrected pointwise intervals are [−0.0024, 0.0228] for the AUROC contrast and [−0.0364, 0.3171] for AP, so this is a descriptive reversal of point estimates, not a confirmed ranking advantage. Semantic interarrival AP also remains below clean AP; threshold failure is a major part of the observed damage, but these data do not show that all damage is confined to the threshold.')
p('The Thursday MLP activity contrast is +0.3115 in Macro-F1, compared with +0.0025 in AUROC and +0.0824 in AP; flag loss yields a negative F1 contrast (−0.0677) alongside a slightly positive AUROC contrast (+0.0029) and negative AP contrast (−0.0050). Similar metric dependence appears in RF: Thursday activity is positive in F1 (+0.0448) but slightly negative in AUROC (−0.0005) and AP (−0.0015). These numerical signs must be interpreted with their full intervals and their scales; tiny ranking differences near an AUROC ceiling are not evidence of a meaningful reversal.')
p('The RT interarrival model contrast persists in the ranking outcomes. Median-imputed RF has semantic-minus-uniform differences −0.1006 in Macro-F1, −0.0088 in AUROC and −0.0210 in AP; clean MLP has +0.0970, +0.0345 and +0.0512, respectively. Thus the RF/MLP direction contrast on RT cannot be explained solely by a fixed 0.5 threshold. Conversely, the Thursday results show that a large fixed-threshold contrast need not imply a similarly large ranking contrast. Reporting the three outcomes together distinguishes feature-dependent ranking changes from operating-point failure without identifying a single causal mechanism.')
h('5 Discussion')
p('The main finding is heterogeneity by model and outage family. Equalizing the number of unavailable columns does not establish equal information loss. Uniform random subsets can produce either optimistic or pessimistic comparisons for a particular family, and the sign can reverse across architectures. The original four adjusted findings, including one borderline result, should be read in that context. Replacement-point and single-column diagnostics provide a limited model-level explanation, while unchanged activity F1 must not be treated as evidence of constant inputs or universally robust detection. Training-only information and correlation controls address specific alternative explanations, but residual mismatch and unmeasured higher-order dependencies prevent a causal semantic interpretation.')
p('The baseline comparison distinguishes the ability to accept NaN from the ability to retain detection performance under an unseen missing pattern. Native histogram boosting avoids arbitrary imputed constants but does not by itself recover missing information. Training exposure to missingness improves some fixed configurations. Reference-capped kNN also performs strongly in the primary outage average, but its restricted reference set and standardized representation are part of the method and must be retained when reproducing its scores.')
p('The results do not establish masking as a universal solution. RF_mask improves several family scores, yet MLP_mask retains a significant payload deficit against its own matched-random control. Noise training improves heavy unbounded-noise performance for the examples reported, yet RF_noise remains sensitive to semantic payload loss. Robustness to one corruption distribution can leave another information-loss mechanism exposed. An operational choice would additionally require attack-specific costs, prevalence and alert handling constraints, which are not optimized here.')
p('Probability analysis adds an operating perspective. An AUROC close to one can coexist with threshold failures, while post-hoc calibration can improve one configuration and worsen another under corruption. Selecting Youden J on clean calibration data is a documented comparison rule, not an optimal operational alert threshold. Future evaluation should consider independently specified false-alarm costs and calibration data representing the anticipated damaged state, without adapting on the final test labels.')
h('6 Limitations')
p('Semantic families are inferred from documented names and do not prove a common deployed failure source. Real outages can affect categorical fields, derived features outside the selected group, data availability timing, or labels. The Gaussian and projection operators do not impose all flow constraints. The study removes exact duplicate input-target pairs and excludes identifiers and categorical predictors, changing the evaluated population. Conflicting labels are retained and grouped, but session and host dependence remain possible.')
p('The 12 primary seeds combine partition and training randomness. Their sets overlap; the corrected resampled t approximation and Holm adjustment provide a cautious finite comparison, not exact site-level uncertainty. No independent multi-site dataset collection is used. The matching design and mechanism analyses are post hoc; the third dataset has not been evaluated, and no preregistration is claimed. The original three constructions share Tuesday FTP/benign intervals; the Thursday replication adds web attack periods within the same benchmark infrastructure. Neither capture is contemporary or comprehensive CICIDS2017 attack coverage. No numeric cross-dataset feature transfer is attempted.')
p('Fixed hyperparameters, capped training/test sizes, the 256-reference kNN budget, short iterative-imputation budget, and neural early stopping limit architecture-level interpretation. Augmented neural training is not update matched. HistGB is the executed native-NaN boosting baseline; XGBoost and a 1D-CNN are not executed. MaskAug full-text access and a faithful implementation remain unresolved. Bibliographic metadata checking should not be interpreted as full reading of every cited paper.')
p('The Thursday test additionally confounds time, attack subtype and low prevalence: training attacks are brute-force, calibration includes XSS, and test attacks are XSS/SQL at 2.2% prevalence. Its twelve seeds use fixed stratified counts and overlapping samples of one chronological construction; their spread is conditional sampling and fitting variability, not uncertainty over attack episodes or operational prevalence. The attack row identities differ despite identical counts. Near all-benign MLP predictions under some outages make threshold behavior an essential qualification. Within-test outage contrasts remain computable, but their size cannot be generalized to temporal change alone or to deployment robustness. The payload-membership diagnostic is also post hoc and nonrandomized, and its residual effect prevents attribution to only the two selected columns.')
h('7 Conclusion')
p('This study characterizes model-dependent sensitivity to feature family outages using size-matched random controls and additional training-only information and correlation controls. The original twelve-partition RT-IoT2022 analysis has four adjusted findings among 48 contrasts, with mixed directions elsewhere. Separate-day corrected-capture replication broadens the tested attack periods while remaining within a laboratory benchmark. The evidence supports reporting threshold and ranking outcomes together, alongside the full effect distribution, matching quality, predictor variability and uncertainty; it does not establish a universal random-outage bias or isolate a causal semantic effect.')
h('Declarations')
p('Author information: Mazin Mohammed Hamid, Northern Technical University, Iraq. Correspondence: mazin.mohammed@ntu.edu.iq. ')
p('Funding: This research received no dedicated funding. Competing interests: The author declares no competing interests.')
p('Ethics and consent: The study uses public benchmark network data and introduces no new participant recruitment or intervention. No institutional ethics exemption or approval is claimed. Reuse remains subject to the original dataset terms.')
p('Data and software availability: Supplementary Tables S1–S12 are supplied in a separate Word document accompanying this manuscript. Source code is publicly available at https://github.com/mazinalswady-glitch/IDS-Robustness- (initial code deposit: commit fa9611f911d5d12e7835a4f28e4e47b085b472a7). Full electronic metric records, split indices, figures and source verification metadata are supplied as Supplementary Software. Original datasets are available from the cited official providers and are not redistributed here.')
p('Use of AI assistance: AI assistance was used to draft and revise text, prepare analysis code, and organize reported outputs. Experimental results are computed by the supplied scripts. The disclosure identifies the assistance used; scientific interpretation and submission responsibility remain with the named author.')
